/ref 17592186216669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186216670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186216671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186216672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216668 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216668 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216668 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d1285df3-060a-5fc6-be97-37ff5f0e6f8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216669 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216669 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216669 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ccbbbe03-535c-5a98-8643-6d9012b867e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216670 #edb/ref 1080 "(x)/(q(x))" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216670 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216670 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c9ab3c9-c802-5710-bfdc-a8bbd8c831cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216671 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216671 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216671 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4eb0a8b5-e74e-55d8-9b13-0e8abbe8a521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216672 #edb/ref 1080 "- (q(x))/(x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216672 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216672 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "70541b3e-71c2-5ccf-a23f-0f664382f814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217003 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21868" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217003 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nx^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6884f33-97b6-502e-91c8-bdd3a4fc95d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217003 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217004 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217004 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4.$ What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bac67ae0-dc3c-528c-9e07-7a81caa86db4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217004 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217005 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217005 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\ny''+4y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3.$ Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "008cfc63-46d4-5e99-84a2-76df45a51001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217005 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=-3$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -3 & =C{e}^{-8} \\\\ C & =-3{e}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=-3e^{8}e^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSince $-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\\neq 0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we conclude that ![](images/1e/1e26515be7d7b9ecf5cd5e1464989f6d90098247bdd95dfae75fd2545fd9bd18.png)\n\nSo, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are ![](images/63/6397adc2a5f64c899725f85822627697055696c0898ffb6e2ee1d2417e802caa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186