6045417] [17592186217800 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=2.$ What is $W(4)?$\n\n$W(4)=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217800 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ed9527e-50d0-53be-a3d3-45d4754a6c02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217800 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{3\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{3} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(4) & =2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3\\cdot 4}=2{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217801 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217801 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217801 #edb/ref 1071 "$y^{″} + \\frac{1}{x}y^{′} + Q(x)y = 0, x > 0$\nLet $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0, ∞)$. The Wronskian satisfies $W(2) = 0$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n$-\\frac{1}{x}$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217801 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72291c8c-7d08-5518-8b04-62e5d8790c3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218195 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218195 #edb/ref 1061 600667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218195 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7527729-b857-5e9b-bea8-a7cbd99e635d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218195 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186218358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218196 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218196 #edb/ref 1061 600668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218196 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb9b873b-720d-52a3-af19-21b0736163e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218196 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218197 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218197 #edb/ref 1061 600671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218197 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21049594-a386-58b7-948b-933fb27c4926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218197 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218198 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218198 #edb/ref 1061 600674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218198 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a5dec47-1b73-5c78-99d1-b02f62ec0319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218198 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218199 #edb/ref 1061 600678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "888a5fbb-9e7e-52b5-8de1-71d906dc8972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186218359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218200 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218200 #edb/ref 1061 600679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218200 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f86b886d-8ad5-5177-85d6-59b75ecbb221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218200 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186218360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218358 #edb/ref 1064 "The Wronskian is a quick way to check whether two solutions of a homogeneous ODE are linearly independent.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn an identity, which tells us how the Wronskian changes with $x$ for an equation of the form \n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the second-order linear homogeneous ODE above on some interval $I.$ Let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & {y}_{2}(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'}(x) & {y}_{2}^{'}(x)\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nThen, **Abel's identity** states that the Wronskian satisfies the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nSolving this equation via separation of variables gives the general form for $W(x){:}$\n\n$$\nW(x) = C e^{-\\int P(x) \\, \\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to determine the behavior of the Wronskian without knowing the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2.$\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''+3xy'+e^x y=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $y_1$ and $y_2,$ and the corresponding Wronskian $W(x).$ Let's determine the differential equation that $W(x)$ must satisfy.\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{'}(x) & =-3xW(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the equation is not already in standard form, we should rewrite it before applying Abel’s identity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218358 #edb/ref 1065 14746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218358 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "df1133ef-ff8b-5e3c-bbce-c0f1a484db10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218358 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218359 #edb/ref 1064 "*Abel's identity* says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their *Wronskian* $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-p(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nTo prove it, we start with the definition\n\n$$\nW(x)=y_1y_2'-y_2y_1'\n$$\n\nand differentiate both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{'}{y}_{2}^{'}}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{''}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{'}{y}_{1}^{'}}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{''} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{''}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{''}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny_1''=-p(x)y_1'-q(x)y_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2''=-p(x)y_2'-q(x)y_2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression for $W'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{'}(x) & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{''}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{''} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(-p(x){y}_{2}^{'}-q(x){y}_{2})-{y}_{2}(-p(x){y}_{1}^{'}-q(x){y}_{1}) \\\\  & =-p(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}}+p(x){y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}+\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}} \\\\  & =-p(x)({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}) \\\\  & =-p(x)W(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W'(x)=-p(x)W(x)$, as required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218359 #edb/ref 1065 14753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218359 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1263afb-6125-5c5f-8c88-5025fd9457db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218359 #edb/ref 1067 "The Proof of Abel's Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218360 #edb/ref 1064 "Because the exponential factor in Abel's identity is *never zero*, we can deduce the following facts:\n\n- If $W(x_0)=0$ at some point $x_0 \\in I,$ then $W(x) = 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\n- If $W(x_0)\\neq 0,$ then $W(x) \\neq 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\nLet's see how this works on concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218360 #edb/ref 1065 14754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218360 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1ff5ce47-4514-52a4-9f42-103d6b73b928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218360 #edb/ref 1067 "Applications of Abel's Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315949 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315949 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315949 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "95dea895-ae65-5d43-951c-870afd31fe30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316341 #edb/ref 1080 "2{e}^{-10}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316341 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316341 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dbe95508-90a4-5739-800e-6730fc486775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317078 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317078 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317078 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317078 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317078 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de9f196c-0661-5c94-9e3f-43c39b8d82e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317119 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317119 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317119 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317119 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317119 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f0a34654-f2e5-50e3-ab8b-574824d4527e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317381 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317381 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317381 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317381 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=2.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317381 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "283b979f-8c0c-5af7-8c3b-c602adbe88b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317381 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-5W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-5W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-5\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-5x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-5x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-5\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-5} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5\\cdot 3}=2{e}^{-10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317384 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317384 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317384 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+(1+x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=1.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04fad153-de50-519c-b7c7-6082be26f701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317384 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & ={x}^{3}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{{1}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =Ce \\\\ C & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)={e}^{-1}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & ={e}^{-1}{e}^{{2}^{3}}={e}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365893 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365893 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365893 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365893 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365893 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2a42bbe3-769a-5a38-9e0d-7251654af238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365894 #edb/ref 1080 "2{e}^{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365894 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365894 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8c388af8-c41d-5c6d-8140-460cd30fd123" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365897 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365897 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365897 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365897 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365897 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365897 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(1)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(1)=0,$ we obtai